/-! # Finite presentation of a closed subspace and its structure quotient

Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms of Spaces, Tag 084Q,
lemma-closed-immersion-finite-presentation; Descent on Spaces, Tag 0610;
Modules on Sites, Tag 082T
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-closed-structure-finite-presentation).
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- For a closed immersion, local finite presentation, finite presentation, finite type
of its kernel ideal, and finite presentation of its direct-image structure module are
equivalent. No Noetherian, quasi-compactness or separation assumption on X is needed.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 084Q, lemma-closed-immersion-finite-presentation;
Tag 0610, lemma-finite-finitely-presented-module; Tag 082T,
lemma-kernel-surjection-finite-onto-finite-presentation
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-closed-structure-finite-presentation).

Proof:
1. A closed immersion is proper by lem-sfb-space-closed-subspace-proper, hence
quasi-compact and separated. Its diagonal is a closed immersion and is therefore
quasi-compact as well. Thus local finite presentation and finite presentation agree.
2. By lem-sfb-space-closed-immersion-quasi-coherent-modules, the structure quotient
and its kernel are quasi-coherent, and the structure map is surjective. On an affine
etale chart Spec R, lem-sfb-space-closed-ideal-correspondence and
lem-sfb-space-qcoh-affine-chart-calculus identify its map with R to R/J and its kernel
with J, not J/J^2.
3. The affine closed immersion is locally finitely presented exactly when J is finitely
generated: use lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation and
lem-weil-pbc-finite-presentation-surjection-kernel-fg with zero polynomial variables
in one direction, and the explicit zero-variable presentation of R/J in the other.
4. Use lem-sfb-space-local-properties-chart-test and the affine chart calculus to
pass between this affine criterion and finite type of the kernel on X. In the forward
direction include any chosen affine chart in the atlas U disjoint-union V_0. In the
reverse direction take an atlas which is the disjoint union of affine opens of V_0;
the affine presentations witness local finite presentation at every source point.
5. If J is generated by a_1,...,a_m, the exact sequence R^m to R to R/J to zero
makes R/J finitely presented. Conversely apply
lem-trf-finite-presentation-surjective-module-kernel to R onto R/J. The affine chart
calculus globalizes the equivalence of finite type of J and finite presentation of
the quotient. Combine this with steps 1 and 4. -/
theorem closedImmersion_structure_finitePresentation_iff
    {S : Scheme.{u}} {Z X : AlgebraicSpace S} (i : Z ⟶ X)
    (hi : IsRepresentableWith (fun _ _ p ↦ IsClosedImmersion p.left) i.hom.hom) :
    (MapIsLocallyOfFinitePresentation i ↔
      (MapIsLocallyOfFinitePresentation i ∧ MapIsQuasiCompact i.hom.hom ∧
        MapIsQuasiCompact (pullback.diagonal i.hom.hom))) ∧
    (MapIsLocallyOfFinitePresentation i ↔
      SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType (kernel (moduleStructureMap i))) ∧
    (SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType (kernel (moduleStructureMap i)) ↔
      SheafOfModules.IsFinitePresentation
        ((moduleDirectImage i).obj (SheafOfModules.unit Z.structureSheaf))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
